<h2 class="header_two">The Bridge Still to Be Built</h2>

<p>Building Bridges reinforced my conviction that the relationship between Nature and economic activity is entering an important period of institutional discovery.</p>

<p>We are making progress in understanding ecological dependencies, evaluating impacts, identifying risks and mobilising finance. All of this is necessary.</p>

<p>But alongside these advances lies another question:</p>

<blockquote>Can our economic institutions recognise not only what Nature is worth, but also the contributions people make towards maintaining and restoring the shared ecological value on which we all depend?</blockquote>

<p>Can that recognition become economically meaningful without converting Nature, or its services, into something someone must own or have the right to trade?</p>

<p>Answering these questions requires more than a financial product. It calls for institutional relationships between evidence, recognition, economic participation and legitimate governance.</p>

<p>The white paper offers one possible starting point, not a finished answer.</p>

<p>Perhaps the next bridge we need to build is not simply between sustainability and finance, but <strong>between contributions to our shared ecological inheritance and everyday economic participation.</strong></p>

<p>And perhaps building it will help bring our economies into a more durable balance with Nature.</p>

<div class="cta-box">
  <h3>From shared ecological value to economic agency</h3>
  <p>My forthcoming white paper, <em>From Shared Ecological Value to Economic Agency: Extending the Value Architecture of Market Economies</em>, explores the missing institutional transmission channel between contributions to shared ecological value and usable economic capacity. MyDIO is presented as an initial practical application and a subject for independent testing, not a proven final answer.</p>
</div>

<div class="framing-box">
  <h3>Key concepts</h3>
  <p><strong>Shared ecological value</strong><br>The benefits of functioning ecological systems, often enjoyed collectively rather than held as an individual property right.</p>
  <p><strong>Economic agency</strong><br>Usable capacity to participate in economic activity, including the possibility of making choices that would otherwise be financially constrained.</p>
  <p><strong>Transmission channel</strong><br>An institutional mechanism connecting qualified contributions, credible recognition and economic acceptance without requiring ownership or trade in the underlying ecological value.</p>
  <p><strong>Independent governance</strong><br>Arrangements through which evidence, qualification and recognition can be scrutinised and their rules developed with legitimate participation beyond the originating business.</p>
</div>

